  "summary": "A church design with a central focus, often circular or polygonal, radiating symmetry.",
  "description": "Features a central space covered by a dome or vault, with arms radiating outward, creating a balanced, geometric composition. This form emphasized the symbolic centrality of the divine and was particularly favored in Byzantine architecture, influencing later Renaissance designs.",
  "culturalContext": "The centrally-planned church — round, octagonal or cross-in-square — descends from Roman mausolea and Byzantine practice, matured in the Greek-cross churches of Constantinople and the Balkans, and reached its Renaissance apotheosis in Brunelleschi's Pazzi Chapel and da Vinci's ideal-church drawings. Its geometry argued theology: the dome as heaven over a unified congregation, versus the processional Latin cross.",
